---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.3 — String Traversal: The ScrollCipher &amp; AP String Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.8, 2.10 · 90-Minute Design-First Build.</strong> String traversal is one of
    the highest-weight topics on the AP Computer Science A exam. Because <code>charAt()</code> and
    the <code>Character</code> class are <strong>not</strong> on the
    <a href="../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a>, every AP String algorithm
    visits characters with a loop and combines <code>length()</code>, <code>substring(...)</code>,
    <code>indexOf(...)</code>, and <code>equals(...)</code>.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Design-First Workflow (One-Shot Challenge):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/ScrollCipher.java" download>ScrollCipher.java</a> — starter file
            containing method Javadocs, stubs, and a built-in <strong>Arcane Scroll Decoder
            Bench</strong> in <code>main()</code>.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">
        <strong>How to work today:</strong>
    </p>
    <ol style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
        <li><strong>Design before coding:</strong> For each method below, read why the game needs
            it and what each parameter carries, then answer the Guided Design questions to choose
            your loop structure, local variables, step order, and edge-case guards.</li>
        <li><strong>Draft &amp; test in the sandbox:</strong> Draft your method code in the box,
            click <strong>Copy</strong> to paste it into <code>ScrollCipher.java</code>, and test
            your decoder live with:</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>javac ScrollCipher.java
java ScrollCipher</code></pre>
    <p style="margin-top:0.4rem; margin-bottom:0;">
        <strong>Step 3 (One-Shot Pass):</strong> Once your sandbox outputs look right on every edge
        case, unlock <code>ScrollCipherCheck.java</code> at the bottom of this page and aim to pass
        all <strong>18 checks</strong> on your very first run.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · Lookahead String Scan</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>countAdjacentDoubles(String spell)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            In the RPG's Spell Validator, stuttered incantations (like <code>"bookkeeper"</code> or
            <code>"abyssal"</code>) carry bonus resonance for every adjacent pair of identical
            runes. Instead of scanning one character at a time, this helper compares each character
            to its right-hand neighbor and counts every matching pair.
        </p>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>String spell</code> (precondition: <code>spell != null</code>) — the
                incantation text to inspect, which may be empty (<code>""</code>) or 1 character long.</li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>int</code>):</strong> the number of starting indices
                <code>i</code> where the 1-character substring at <code>i</code> equals the
                1-character substring at <code>i + 1</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>"bookkeeper"</code> &rarr; <code>3</code> (pairs <code>"oo"</code> at 1–2,
                <code>"kk"</code> at 3–4, and <code>"ee"</code> at 5–6).</li>
            <li><code>"aaa"</code> &rarr; <code>2</code> (pairs at indices <code>0–1</code> and
                <code>1–2</code> overlap, and both count!).</li>
            <li><code>"dragon"</code> &rarr; <code>0</code> (no identical neighbors).</li>
            <li><code>""</code> or <code>"x"</code> &rarr; <code>0</code> (fewer than two
                characters means zero pairs exist, and it must not crash).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>countAdjacentDoubles</code></h3>
        <p>Think through the architecture before writing code:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Loop Choice &amp; Bounds:</strong> Should we use a <code>for</code> loop or a
                <code>while</code> loop here, and why? Since the loop body inspects both
                <code>i</code> and <code>i + 1</code> (calling <code>substring(i + 1, i + 2)</code>),
                what must the stopping condition be so index <code>i + 1</code> never steps past the
                end of <code>spell</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>Local Variables &amp; Comparison:</strong> What local variable do you need
                before the loop, what value does it start at, and why must you compare two
                1-character substrings with <code>.equals(...)</code> instead of <code>==</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>Edge Cases:</strong> Trace what your chosen loop bound does automatically when
                <code>spell</code> is <code>""</code> (length <code>0</code>) or <code>"x"</code>
                (length <code>1</code>)—do you need a separate <code>if</code> guard at the top, or
                does the loop bound already handle both safely?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>ScrollCipher.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>countAdjacentDoubles</code> method body based on your design
                above, then copy it into <code>ScrollCipher.java</code> and run
                <code>java ScrollCipher</code> to verify <code>"bookkeeper"</code>,
                <code>"aaa"</code>, <code>"x"</code>, and <code>""</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 2 · Accumulating a New String</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>reverseRunes(String scroll)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Guild messengers scramble secret dispatches by writing them mirror-backward. Because
            Java <code>String</code> objects are <strong>immutable</strong> (you cannot swap
            characters inside the original string), <code>reverseRunes</code> constructs and returns
            a brand-new <code>String</code> one character at a time.
        </p>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>String scroll</code> (precondition: <code>scroll != null</code>) — the text to
                reverse (may be <code>""</code> or a single character).</li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>String</code>):</strong> a new string with the exact
                characters of <code>scroll</code> in reverse order.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>"dragon"</code> &rarr; <code>"nogard"</code> (index <code>5</code> down to
                index <code>0</code>).</li>
            <li><code>"A"</code> &rarr; <code>"A"</code>, and <code>""</code> &rarr;
                <code>""</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>reverseRunes</code></h3>
        <p>Plan the accumulator and traversal boundaries before coding:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Similarities &amp; Differences:</strong> How is building a reversed
                <code>String</code> similar to an integer sum accumulator from Lesson 4.2, and what
                local variable (and initial value) replaces <code>int total = 0;</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>Traversal Bounds:</strong> If you walk backward using a <code>for</code>
                loop, what is the exact starting index (<code>scroll.length()</code> vs.
                <code>scroll.length() - 1</code>), what comparison keeps index <code>0</code>
                included, and how does the loop behave on <code>""</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>ScrollCipher.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>reverseRunes</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>ScrollCipher.java</code>, and check the sandbox output for
                <code>"dragon"</code>, <code>"A"</code>, and <code>""</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Alphabet Lookup &amp; Wrap-Around Math</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>shiftUppercaseCipher(String text, int shift)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            To protect patrol orders, the guild uses a Caesar cipher across the 26 uppercase letters:
        </p>
        <pre><code>String alpha = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ";</code></pre>
        <p>
            One method handles <strong>both encryption and decryption</strong>: passing a positive
            <code>shift</code> (like <code>+3</code>) moves forward in the alphabet, while passing a
            negative <code>shift</code> (like <code>-3</code>) shifts backward to decode the scroll.
            Spaces and punctuation must pass through unchanged so sentences stay readable.
        </p>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>String text</code> (precondition: <code>text != null</code>) — message
                containing uppercase letters and possibly spaces, digits, or punctuation.</li>
            <li><code>int shift</code> — signed shift amount (can be positive, <code>0</code>,
                negative, or larger than <code>26</code>).</li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>String</code>):</strong> a new string where each uppercase
                <code>A–Z</code> character is shifted modulo <code>26</code> and every other
                character is preserved as-is.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>("DRAGON Z", 3)</code> &rarr; <code>"GUDJRQ C"</code> (<code>Z</code> at index
                <code>25</code> wraps around <code>0..25</code> to <code>C</code> at index
                <code>2</code>; space stays <code>" "</code>).</li>
            <li><code>("ABZ!", -2)</code> &rarr; <code>"YZX!"</code> (<code>A</code> at index
                <code>0</code> wraps backward 2 steps to <code>Y</code> at index <code>24</code>;
                <code>!</code> stays <code>"!"</code>).</li>
            <li><code>("HAL", 27)</code> &rarr; <code>"IBM"</code> (shifting by <code>27</code>
                wraps identically to <code>+1</code>).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>shiftUppercaseCipher</code></h3>
        <p>Trace the lookup logic and wrap-around edge cases before coding:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Local Variables &amp; Non-Letter Guard:</strong> Inside a forward
                <code>for</code> loop over <code>text</code>, after you extract <code>ch</code> and
                run <code>int pos = alpha.indexOf(ch);</code>, what value of <code>pos</code> tells
                you that <code>ch</code> is a space, digit, or punctuation mark rather than an
                uppercase letter—and what should you append in that case?</li>
            <li><strong>Negative Modulo Edge Case:</strong> In Java, <code>(0 + (-2)) % 26</code>
                evaluates to <code>-2</code> (not <code>24</code>!), which would crash
                <code>alpha.substring(...)</code>. Why does the two-step formula
                <code>((pos + shift) % 26 + 26) % 26</code> work safely for <em>both</em> negative
                shifts like <code>-2</code> (or <code>-55</code>) and positive wrap-arounds like
                <code>pos = 25, shift = 3</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>ScrollCipher.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>shiftUppercaseCipher</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>ScrollCipher.java</code>, and run <code>java ScrollCipher</code> to verify that
                <code>"GUDJRQ C"</code> with shift <code>-3</code> decodes back to
                <code>"DRAGON Z"</code> and <code>"ABZ!"</code> with <code>-2</code> gives
                <code>"YZX!"</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · AP FRQ Synthesis: Dynamic Substring Splicing</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>purgeCursedGlyph(String scroll, String glyph)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Before a hero reads a recovered scroll, the purifier must strip out every occurrence of
            a cursed rune sequence (<code>glyph</code>). Sometimes removing one occurrence causes the
            remaining left and right halves to snap together and form a <em>brand-new</em> cursed
            glyph! Removing substrings cleanly until none remain is one of the most classic AP CS A
            FRQ 1 String problems.
        </p>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>String scroll</code> (precondition: <code>scroll != null</code>) — original
                scroll text.</li>
            <li><code>String glyph</code> (precondition: <code>glyph != null</code> and
                <code>glyph.length() &gt; 0</code>) — non-empty target substring to erase.</li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>String</code>):</strong> <code>scroll</code> with every
                occurrence of <code>glyph</code> repeatedly spliced out until <code>glyph</code>
                no longer appears anywhere.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>("hexed sword of hexes", "hex")</code> &rarr; <code>"ed sword of es"</code>.</li>
            <li><code>("booook", "oo")</code> &rarr; <code>"bk"</code> (removing the first
                <code>"oo"</code> leaves <code>"book"</code>, which creates a new
                <code>"oo"</code> that must also be removed!).</li>
            <li><code>("fireball", "zzz")</code> &rarr; <code>"fireball"</code> (never enters loop;
                returns unchanged).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>purgeCursedGlyph</code></h3>
        <p>Compare loop strategies and trace the string splice:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong><code>for</code> vs. <code>while</code> Loop Choice:</strong> Why is a
                single left-to-right <code>for</code> loop over indices the wrong tool for
                <code>purgeCursedGlyph("xaabby", "ab")</code>, whereas a <code>while</code> loop
                checking <code>scroll.indexOf(glyph)</code> catches chained formations? What exact
                <code>while</code> condition stops the loop once <code>glyph</code> is gone?</li>
            <li><strong>Local Index &amp; Splice Formula:</strong> Inside the loop, once you locate
                <code>int idx = scroll.indexOf(glyph);</code>, what are the two
                <code>substring</code> calls that join everything strictly <em>before</em>
                <code>idx</code> with everything strictly <em>after</em> the matched
                <code>glyph</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>ScrollCipher.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>purgeCursedGlyph</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>ScrollCipher.java</code>, and run <code>java ScrollCipher</code> to inspect all
                four parts before unlocking <code>ScrollCipherCheck.java</code> below?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>All four methods in <code>ScrollCipher.java</code> run cleanly in the <code>java ScrollCipher</code> sandbox</li>
            <li>You unlock <code>ScrollCipherCheck.java</code> below and aim for <strong><code>18 passed, 0 failed</code></strong> on your first run</li>
            <li>You have downloaded your <code>.json</code> answers file for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/string-loops.html">Loops + Strings</a> — traversal, reversing, substring patterns</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a> — String methods on the exam</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
